Burnoff

Client-side Sentinel-2 SWIR flare detection with peer-to-peer sync.

  Browser                              Browser (peer)
 +---------------------+              +---------------------+
 |  Map + UI           |   WebRTC /   |  Map + UI           |
 |  LWW-Map CRDT <------+-- WS sync ->+---> LWW-Map CRDT   |
 |  +---------------+  |              |  +---------------+  |
 |  | detect        |  |              |  | detect        |  |
 |  +-------+-------+  |              |  +-------+-------+  |
 +---------+|-----------+              +---------+|-----------+
            | HTTP range requests                |
            v                                    v
      Element84 STAC -- Sentinel-2 L2A COGs

Open the map, navigate to an area of interest, select date quarters,
click Detect. The app downloads Sentinel-2 imagery and runs the
detection algorithm entirely in your browser. Results sync between
all open sessions via WebRTC -- when you detect, idle peers
automatically split the work.


Quick Start

  make serve     # Dev server on :8000 + signaling on :4444


How It Works

Gas flares emit strongly in shortwave infrared. Burnoff reads
cloud-optimised GeoTIFF bands (B12, B11, B8A) from Element84's STAC
catalogue via windowed HTTP range requests, runs the shared s2-flares
detector client-side in a Web Worker, and clusters detections across
dates using anchor-based spatial merging. The detector, clustering and
quality score are the s2-flares methodology core, vendored as a git
submodule at web/vendor/s2-flares (github.com/data-desk-eco/s2-flares).

Detection pipeline (per 256x256 pixel block):
  1. Cloud screening via SCL band
  2. DN to surface reflectance, background median for contrast threshold
  3. Fused brightness + contrast + thermal filter pass
  4. Connected components, cluster quality filters, warm-region halo rejection
  5. Cross-date clustering (grid-indexed, anchor-based, configurable radius)

Each cluster carries a vision-validated quality score (display-only;
the avg-B12 slider stays the active gate): a smooth B12/B11-ratio term,
a clear-sky persistence term, and a geometric glint penalty. Clusters
with a strongly negative glint penalty -- high-sun specular geometry
across every look, no flame spectral evidence -- are flagged as
possible sun glint. This replaces the old seasonal-window heuristic and,
deriving from sun elevation, works on synced detections too.

P2P sync: results are stored in a custom LWW-Map CRDT, persisted
locally via IndexedDB, and synced across peers via WebRTC
DataChannels with a custom binary sync protocol.


Project Structure

  web/
    app.js              Map viewer, CRDT sync, cross-date clustering + score UI
    detect-worker.js    Web Worker driving the s2-flares detector per block
    crdt.js             LWW-Map CRDT with binary codec
    sync.js             Sync protocol, awareness, validation
    rtc.js              WebRTC DataChannel mesh
    store.js            IndexedDB persistence
    vnf.js              VIIRS Nightfire overlay
    terminals.geojson   LNG terminal locations (Global Energy Monitor)
    index.html          Entry point
    style.css           UI styles
    vendor/s2-flares    Shared detection/clustering/score core (git submodule)
  signal/
    worker.js           Cloudflare Durable Object signaling (production)
    server.js           WebSocket signaling relay for local dev (zero deps)
  test/                 Determinism (incl. score) + P2P tests

Zero npm dependencies. MapLibre GL and geotiff.js are vendored. The
detector, clustering, quality score and UTM projection come from the
s2-flares submodule; everything else -- CRDT, WebRTC mesh, sync
protocol, IndexedDB persistence, signaling -- uses browser and
Node.js builtins only.


References

Faruolo et al. (2024) The DAFI v2 algorithm for gas flare detection
https://doi.org/10.1088/1748-9326/ad82fb
